\section{Choosing an input}{

Use a fitted ordinary model for fit-level views. Use saved G/D studies,
information, DIF/DFF, bias, QC or PCA results for their own \code{plot()} methods.
Their public help pages explain the view controls, returned tables and
interpretation: \code{\link[=plot.mfrm_information]{plot.mfrm_information()}}, \code{\link[=plot.mfrm_dif]{plot.mfrm_dif()}},
\code{\link[=plot.mfrm_dif_interaction]{plot.mfrm_dif_interaction()}}, \code{\link[=plot.mfrm_anchor_drift]{plot.mfrm_anchor_drift()}},
\code{\link[=plot.mfrm_qc_pipeline]{plot.mfrm_qc_pipeline()}}, \code{\link[=plot.mfrm_bias]{plot.mfrm_bias()}}, \code{\link[=plot.mfrm_residual_pca]{plot.mfrm_residual_pca()}}
and \code{\link[=plot.mfrm_diagnostics]{plot.mfrm_diagnostics()}}. \code{\link[=plot.mfrm_population_intervals]{plot.mfrm_population_intervals()}} adds
saved latent-regression coefficients and contrasts. Start with these pages
for saved results.
A common function name does not make every view available for every model.
Two-family GPCM and corrected JML retain their separate supported result
routes. Plotting does not add intervals, repair unavailable values or equate
scales. Use original result objects rather than unclassed lists or CSV tables.
}

\section{Single-fit views}{

\code{plot(fit)} still draws the ordinary Wright map. New \code{type} choices include
\code{reliability}, \code{agreement}, \code{person_fit}, \code{rater_severity}, \code{thresholds},
\code{residual_qq}, \code{residual_matrix}, \code{residual_pca}, \code{local_dependence},
\code{marginal_fit}, \code{marginal_pairwise}, \code{fair_average}, \code{displacement},
\code{facets_chisq}, \code{bubble}, \code{guttman}, \code{unexpected}, \code{qc}, \code{apa_summary},
\code{bias}, \code{facet_equivalence} and \code{facet_dashboard}.

Use \code{metric} for the quantity and \code{view} for a display variant. Reliability
accepts \code{reliability}, \code{separation} or \code{strata}. Agreement accepts \code{exact}
or \code{correlation}, with \code{view = "pairs"} (default) or \code{"heatmap"};
\code{metric = "difference"} applies only to pairs. Severity separation is
not agreement. The former \code{plot_type} argument becomes \code{view} for marginal
fit, fair averages, displacement, facet chi-square, unexpected ratings and
facet dashboards. Keep other named controls from the legacy parameter help.

Explicit controls are passed only to the chosen renderer; omitted controls
retain that renderer's defaults. Inapplicable and conflicting controls are
rejected. Supply matching saved \code{diagnostics} for repeated fit-level views.
Some legacy fit-level convenience routes compute diagnostics when omitted;
this does not imply model refitting. Saved-result plots reuse their results.
}

